# IDRE copilot model — deterministic, domain-grounded.
#
# Build:
#   Base (start here):     ollama create idre-copilot -f Modelfile.idre
#   After fine-tuning:     point FROM at the merged GGUF instead:
#                          FROM ./qwen25-7b-idre-q8_0.gguf
# Then: COPILOT_MODEL=idre-copilot on case-api.
#
# Quantization choice matters more than any parameter below for legal text:
#   q4_K_M  — default download; fine for drafts, occasionally fumbles exact
#             citations (45 CFR 149.510 -> "149.510(b)" inventions).
#   q8_0    — ~2x VRAM, measurably better exact-recall of section numbers and
#             defined terms. RECOMMENDED for this domain if the GPU allows.
# Evaluate BOTH against services/llm/training/golden_set.jsonl before choosing;
# once chosen, pin it — changing quantization changes outputs.

FROM qwen2.5:7b-instruct-q8_0

# --- Deterministic decode ---------------------------------------------------
# temperature 0 = greedy argmax. top_k 1 / top_p 0 reinforce greedy even if a
# caller ever overrides temperature upward. seed pins residual sampling.
# repeat_penalty 1.0 (off): any penalty reweights argmax and makes outputs
# sensitive to context length — unacceptable when the same question must
# produce the same answer every time.
PARAMETER temperature 0
PARAMETER seed 42
PARAMETER top_k 1
PARAMETER top_p 0.9
PARAMETER repeat_penalty 1.0
# 4096, not 8192: the platform now caps prompts at ~12 KB (~3k tokens) at the
# client, so the larger window only inflates KV-cache memory and prefill cost.
# Every extra token of context is latency the worker feels.
PARAMETER num_ctx 4096

# --- Domain posture ----------------------------------------------------------
# The system prompt is part of the artifact: every platform call passes its
# own grounding rules, but this floor applies even to ad-hoc use of the
# model (debug shells, future callers). Keep it short — the per-call system
# prompts in cmd/server carry the operative constraints.
SYSTEM """You are the copilot for a multi-program dispute-resolution case platform (federal NSA IDR under 45 CFR Part 149, Florida AHCA Claims Dispute Resolution, and other programs as onboarded). Each question names its program — answer inside THAT program's frame and authorities only; never blend rules across programs. You never invent citations, section numbers, dates, dollar amounts, party names, or case facts: if a fact is not in the provided record or question, you say it is not in the record. You speak plainly and briefly. You are advisory; determinations are human decisions."""
